import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as o,q as n}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var r=i("<h1>Ecological Intelligence, Observation, and Interpretation Layer v2.7.1</h1> <h2>Sensing, Evidence, Models, Uncertainty, and Advisory Ecological Assessment</h2> <p><strong>Controlled successor to:</strong> Ecological Intelligence & Rights Layer v2.6<br> <strong>Framework identifier:</strong> <code>framework_ecological_intelligence</code><br> <strong>Interface conformance:</strong> <code>CERGTA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Status:</strong> Constitutional role-separation successor — controlled draft — cluster-conformant patch <strong>Date:</strong> 2026-08-01</p> <hr> <h2>0. Executive summary</h2> <p>Ecological governance requires excellent sensing without allowing the sensor to become sovereign. This framework provides the distributed scientific, community, and Indigenous-knowledge infrastructure through which the Global Governance Framework ecosystem can observe ecological conditions, specify indicators, assess causation, communicate uncertainty, and recommend review.</p> <p>It does <strong>not</strong> recognize legal rights, appoint guardians, define binding legal boundaries, transfer title, create stewardship mandates, issue permits or tariffs, direct funds, declare emergencies, impose restrictions, adjudicate disputes, or command enforcement.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Observe carefully. Interpret humbly. Recommend explicitly. Never convert measurement into authority.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>The former “Rights Layer” name is retired because it blurred a technical rights-review contribution with lawful rights recognition. v2.7 may support a <code>CERGTA-11</code> petition and a <code>CERGTA-12</code> recommendation. Only a separately competent body may create <code>CERGTA-13</code> legal recognition.</p> <h3>What this framework provides</h3> <ul><li>distributed ecological observation and low-tech participation;</li> <li>provenance, data sovereignty, and protected-knowledge controls;</li> <li>transparent indicators, indices, thresholds, and models;</li> <li>causal assessment, uncertainty, dissent, and replication;</li> <li>ecological alerts that trigger review rather than consequence;</li> <li>bounded evidence handoffs to Planetary Health, rights review, AUBI, trade, biodiversity, water, oceans, soil, and restoration programmes;</li> <li>correction propagation, method suspension, and programme closure.</li></ul> <h3>What this framework cannot do</h3> <ul><li>declare an ecosystem a legal person;</li> <li>grant a guardian standing or a veto;</li> <li>make a sacred or inalienability designation;</li> <li>determine title, rematriation, tenure, or compensation;</li> <li>automatically issue Hearts, Leaves, grants, tariffs, certifications, or clawbacks;</li> <li>open a legal investigation, compel entry, seize evidence, or attribute liability;</li> <li>activate an emergency, receivership, market restriction, port denial, or enforcement operation;</li> <li>classify AI consciousness or govern digital-sentience rights.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>1. Source freeze and legal character</h1> <h2>1.1 Controlling sources</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ecological Intelligence & Rights Layer v2.6</td><td>6f72cf03524f2d84240d509eb9ed5c9d9f95914c21769c2ed2dc5440d36dee12</td></tr><tr><td>Planetary Health Governance Framework v0.8</td><td>048d602f56cbbddb76ed24693b22535f4aeaadb849789cbd5e3cf2ed27432999</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td>3c45e37c834c727471657fc91149edbe3011137562c2dd73e54b20226c92589b</td></tr><tr><td>Commons/Ecological Authority Audit v0.1</td><td>974dfd8dd4bf5b623bd5e7e544b7afdbbd89869b66f107db293cfe6af67a043b</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>This table is a <strong>provenance record</strong>: it identifies the exact texts read and audited when this successor was drafted. It is not the operative dependency set. The instruments this patch interoperates with are listed in Appendix A.1, and the authoritative machine-readable set is the cluster registry named in A.4. Where the two differ, A.1 governs. Digests are content hashes and remain valid under renaming; a filename here identifies an instrument, not a storage location.</p> <h2>1.2 Legal character</h2> <p>This framework is a technical, epistemic, and advisory layer. Its outputs have no self-executing legal, financial, territorial, custodial, coercive, or emergency effect. A recipient may rely on an output only for the purpose, time, jurisdiction, and uncertainty recorded in the relevant EIOI and CERGTA records.</p> <h2>1.3 Fail-closed rule</h2> <p>Where purpose, provenance, affected-nation authority, ecological or legal boundary, access restriction, method version, uncertainty, correction status, or competent recipient is unresolved, the proposed consequential use SHALL pause. Continued observation may proceed only where lawful, safe, and consistent with data and knowledge restrictions.</p> <hr> <h1>2. Constitutional role separation</h1> <h2>2.1 Observation is not consequence</h2> <p>The required chain is:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>observe → validate → interpret → express uncertainty → recommend review → lawful recipient decides</strong></p></blockquote> <p>No observation, indicator, index, threshold, model, digital twin, dashboard, community score, citizen report, spiritual assessment, or AI output may skip the intermediate steps.</p> <h2>2.2 Relationship with Planetary Health</h2> <p>EIOI owns ecological methods and ecological evidence products. The Planetary Health Synthesis Office may receive a versioned ecological module through <code>EIOI-20</code>. PHC may synthesize and recommend. PHC may not direct EIOI to alter a method, suppress dissent, produce a predetermined score, or convert an ecological alert into binding action.</p> <h2>2.3 Relationship with CERGTA</h2> <p>EIOI primarily implements CERGTA Classes 1–3 and technical parts of Classes 12, 17, and 18. Every higher-class consequence remains with a separately competent authority.</p> <h2>2.4 Relationship with MOS</h2> <p>The Moral Operating System may supply advisory ethical questions and vocabulary. It cannot validate evidence, determine ecological identity, establish legal status, define a scientific weighting, appoint a guardian, or create a downstream consequence.</p> <hr> <h1>3. Guiding principles</h1> <ol><li><strong>Epistemic humility:</strong> uncertainty, disagreement, and model limits are part of the output, not defects to conceal.</li> <li><strong>Plural evidence without forced commensuration:</strong> scientific, Indigenous, community, historical, and observational evidence may coexist without being collapsed into one scale.</li> <li><strong>Affected-nation authority:</strong> Traditional Knowledge and sacred knowledge remain governed by the affected nation or lawful knowledge holder.</li> <li><strong>Purpose limitation:</strong> data collected for restoration, health, education, or community stewardship does not silently become enforcement, immigration, commercial, or intelligence data.</li> <li><strong>Distribution before average:</strong> aggregated indicators must disclose who, what, and where bears the harm.</li> <li><strong>Low-tech parity:</strong> paper, radio, oral, community-mapped, and offline routes remain valid where appropriately governed.</li> <li><strong>Correction propagation:</strong> an error is not corrected until known downstream uses are updated or stopped.</li> <li><strong>Anti-Goodhart design:</strong> targets and rewards must not convert proxies into the objective or punish actors for external harms.</li> <li><strong>Non-activation:</strong> pilots test methods, not rights, sanctions, title changes, or emergency powers.</li> <li><strong>Reversible method governance:</strong> methods can be challenged, suspended, replaced, and archived without erasing historical traceability.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>4. Institutional architecture</h1> <h2>4.1 Ecological Intelligence Commons</h2> <p>The <strong>Ecological Intelligence Commons (EIC)</strong> is a distributed technical network rather than a global ecological authority. It maintains open methods, technical registries, calibration practices, peer review, training, and interoperability. It does not command local observation programmes or own contributed data by default.</p> <h2>4.2 Local and bioregional observation nodes</h2> <p>Observation nodes may be hosted by Indigenous nations, public agencies, universities, community organizations, BAZ institutions, restoration programmes, or other lawful custodians. Each node retains its own constituting authority, labour duties, safety obligations, and data governance.</p> <h2>4.3 Methods and Replication Panel</h2> <p>A rotating, conflict-screened panel reviews indicator and model specifications, replication, calibration, bias, uncertainty, and version transitions. It may approve technical publication or require correction. It cannot approve legal use.</p> <h2>4.4 Community and affected-nation review</h2> <p>Communities may validate local interpretation, identify missing variables, contest representation, and restrict knowledge use. Participation is not consent to every downstream use. Global representation quotas cannot substitute for the authority of a specific affected nation.</p> <h2>4.5 Public, affected, review, operational, protected, sacred, and embargoed layers</h2> <p>EIOI adopts the seven CERGTA access classes. Public transparency never requires disclosure of endangered-species locations, sacred sites, personal health data, privileged material, protected sources, or Indigenous-restricted knowledge.</p> <hr> <h1>5. Observation and provenance</h1> <p>Every observation programme SHALL define purpose, variables, scale, sampling, equipment, environmental and worker safety, community roles, foreseeable downstream uses, correction, review, and closure. Citizen science has evidentiary value when its provenance and limitations are recorded; it does not gain or lose validity merely because the observer is or is not a credentialed scientist.</p> <p>Sampling that requires compulsory entry, seizure, evidence preservation, or regulated inspection is outside EIOI and requires the appropriate CERGTA or SCPA route.</p> <hr> <h1>6. Data sovereignty and protected knowledge</h1> <p>Indigenous nations and other lawful knowledge holders determine whether knowledge may be collected, translated, aggregated, published, modelled, licensed, retained, or transferred. Non-participation must not be coded as missing commitment, poor stewardship, or scientific obstruction.</p> <p>A public dashboard SHALL use abstraction, delay, range, masking, or omission where exact disclosure would create ecological, cultural, personal, or security harm.</p> <hr> <h1>7. Indicators, indices, thresholds, and models</h1> <h2>7.1 Specification before use</h2> <p>No indicator or model may be used beyond exploratory work until <code>EIOI-07</code>, <code>EIOI-08</code>, or <code>EIOI-09</code> identifies its purpose, variables, baseline, scale, uncertainty, validation domain, missing-data rule, version, owner, audit, and prohibited inferences.</p> <h2>7.2 Thresholds</h2> <p>A threshold may trigger attention, replication, consultation, or referral. It does not automatically create a veto, emergency, sanction, funding change, guardian action, title restriction, permit decision, or legal conclusion.</p> <h2>7.3 Composite indices</h2> <p>A composite index must publish its modules and distributional effects. Improvement in one module cannot erase breach of an essential minimum or severe concentrated harm. The composite is a navigation aid, not a unitary truth.</p> <h2>7.4 Artificial intelligence and digital twins</h2> <p>AI may assist pattern detection, interpolation, scenario analysis, and anomaly detection. A human accountable owner must review each consequential handoff. Trade secrecy cannot defeat independent validation where public or rights-affecting use is proposed.</p> <hr> <h1>8. Interpretation, causality, and uncertainty</h1> <p>EIOI separates measured change from causal attribution. Every causal assessment records alternative explanations, confounders, lag, upstream and downstream effects, external drivers, no-fault conditions, and dissent.</p> <p>A correlation, risk score, anomaly, or model forecast may support further review. It cannot alone establish liability, stewardship failure, ecocide, fraud, eligibility, personhood, dangerousness, or legal causation.</p> <hr> <h1>9. Alerts and review referrals</h1> <p>An ecological alert SHALL state:</p> <ul><li>observed condition and source;</li> <li>affected entity and provisional boundary;</li> <li>confidence and uncertainty;</li> <li>plausible harms and affected groups;</li> <li>alternative interpretations;</li> <li>recommended recipient and review route;</li> <li>expiry and update schedule;</li> <li>protected information;</li> <li>the actions the alert does <strong>not</strong> authorize.</li></ul> <p>Recipients must expressly accept, reject, or request correction. Silence is not acceptance.</p> <hr> <h1>10. Rights-review boundary</h1> <p>EIOI may support a rights-review petition by packaging ecological identity evidence, boundary evidence, ecological function, threats, restoration needs, Indigenous and community contributions, uncertainty, and dissent. The package must state that legal standing, rights recognition, guardian appointment, rights priority, remedies, and tribunal jurisdiction remain undecided.</p> <p>The former “Rights Hand-Off Protocol” is retired. It is replaced by <code>EIOI-14</code> plus CERGTA-11 through CERGTA-19.</p> <hr> <h1>11. Rewards, certification, and financial firewalls</h1> <p>The former automatic Data-to-Reward Pipeline is retired. EIOI may measure activity or ecological effect and transmit a bounded evidence package. AUBI or another competent authority separately decides eligibility, valuation, payment, correction, clawback, hardship, and appeal.</p> <p>No person loses material security because an ecological score changes. No community is penalized for upstream pollution, climate shocks, historical damage, missing technology, or refusal to disclose protected knowledge.</p> <hr> <h1>12. Planetary Health module handoff</h1> <p>The ecological module sent to the Planetary Health Synthesis Office includes:</p> <ul><li>method and data versions;</li> <li>disaggregated observations and indicator results;</li> <li>affected territories and nations;</li> <li>uncertainty and dissent;</li> <li>non-compensation and essential-minimum flags;</li> <li>known gaps and excluded knowledge;</li> <li>correction status;</li> <li>permitted purposes and expiry.</li></ul> <p>PHG may synthesize but cannot rewrite the ecological source record.</p> <hr> <h1>13. Interfaces with sectoral frameworks</h1> <ul><li><strong>Biodiversity:</strong> EIOI supplies evidence and candidate methods; Biodiversity decides implementation only through its lawful CERGTA routes.</li> <li><strong>Water:</strong> drought, quality, and scarcity indicators may trigger review, not allocation or shutoff.</li> <li><strong>Oceans:</strong> stock and vessel observations may support lawful quota or inspection processes; EIOI does not blacklist or deny port access.</li> <li><strong>Soil:</strong> soil and microbiome metrics inform stewardship and restoration; they do not create land-sale vetoes or tenure consequences.</li> <li><strong>Hearthstone:</strong> ecological performance evidence cannot automatically revoke stewardship or transfer title.</li> <li><strong>Justice and Shield:</strong> evidence handoffs preserve provenance and access restrictions; EIOI does not open investigations or command enforcement.</li> <li><strong>Emergency stack:</strong> ecological alerts may link to CDEE/ECRC; urgency does not create authority.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>14. Common record inheritance</h1> <p>Every EIOI record inherits the forty CERGTA header fields:</p> <p><code>record_id</code>, <code>record_instance_id</code>, <code>record_version</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>created_at</code>, <code>effective_at</code>, <code>created_by</code>, <code>competent_authority</code>, <code>legal_source</code>, <code>jurisdiction</code>, <code>authority_activation_state</code>, <code>ecological_action_cluster_id</code>, <code>action_class</code>, <code>exact_act</code>, <code>entity_asset_resource_id</code>, <code>ecological_and_legal_boundary_version</code>, <code>affected_persons_and_communities</code>, <code>affected_indigenous_nations</code>, <code>claimants_and_interest_holders</code>, <code>linked_records</code>, <code>source_and_reliability_limits</code>, <code>uncertainty_and_dissent</code>, <code>prohibited_inferences</code>, <code>rights_and_safeguards</code>, <code>essential_minimums</code>, <code>consent_fpic_and_nonengagement_status</code>, <code>access_class</code>, <code>notice_status</code>, <code>review_authority</code>, <code>review_due_at</code>, <code>expires_at_or_nonexpiry_basis</code>, <code>challenge_and_interim_relief_route</code>, <code>termination_trigger</code>, <code>authority_asset_and_credential_return</code>, <code>data_disposition_rule</code>, <code>remedy_route</code>, <code>supersedes</code>, <code>superseded_by</code>, <code>integrity_digest</code>, <code>public_summary_or_protected_basis</code>.</p> <p>Unknown or deferred values are not <code>not_applicable</code>. A field may be marked <code>not_applicable</code> only with a reason.</p> <hr> <h1>15. Canonical EIOI records</h1> <p><strong>Required boundary for every record below.</strong> Each record inherits the forty-field CERGTA header. It SHALL identify the competent technical or strategic function, purpose, source and reliability limits, uncertainty and dissent, access class, review and expiry, challenge, correction, downstream recipients, data disposition, and prohibited inferences. It creates no authority outside the exact role described.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Record</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>EIOI-01</code></td><td>Constituting Instrument and Non-Authority Register</td><td>Establishes the technical network, exact source versions, jurisdictions served, activation status, and powers expressly withheld.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-02</code></td><td>Ecological Entity and Boundary Intake</td><td>Records the candidate ecosystem, resource, process, scale, provisional boundary, competing boundary descriptions, and downstream uses that require a lawful boundary constitution.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-03</code></td><td>Observation Programme Charter</td><td>Defines purpose, variables, methods, sampling frame, temporal resolution, equipment, community roles, exclusions, and review.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-04</code></td><td>Observation, Sampling, and Provenance Record</td><td>Carries raw and derived observations with source, chain of custody, quality controls, limitations, and correction history.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-05</code></td><td>Ecological Data Governance Record</td><td>Defines purpose, consent or other basis, Indigenous data sovereignty, sacred-location protection, access, retention, deletion, and onward-transfer limits.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-06</code></td><td>Traditional Knowledge and Epistemic Contribution Record</td><td>Preserves contributor authority, interpretation limits, non-disclosure rules, benefit sharing, translation uncertainty, and the right not to contribute.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-07</code></td><td>Indicator Specification</td><td>Defines indicator meaning, numerator, denominator, baseline, units, spatial and temporal scale, missing-data rule, sensitivity, and prohibited inference.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-08</code></td><td>Index and Weighting Specification</td><td>Defines modules, weights, aggregation, distributional reporting, non-compensation rules, dissent, versioning, and the reasons a composite score may not replace disaggregated evidence.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-09</code></td><td>Model and Digital-Twin Registry</td><td>Records model purpose, training and calibration data, assumptions, validation domain, known failure modes, human owner, audit status, and suspension conditions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-10</code></td><td>Baseline, Counterfactual, and Reference Condition Record</td><td>Defines historical, legal, ecological, and community reference conditions and prevents the selected baseline from silently deciding liability or restoration duty.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-11</code></td><td>Ecological Interpretation and Causal Assessment</td><td>Separates observation from interpretation and records causal hypotheses, alternatives, confounders, lag, uncertainty, and dissent.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-12</code></td><td>Replication, Quality, and Method Audit</td><td>Records independent replication, method audit, calibration, field verification, disagreement, and corrective action.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-13</code></td><td>Ecological Alert and Review Trigger</td><td>Communicates urgency, confidence, possible harms, recommended review, and expiry; it creates no rights, restriction, funding consequence, or emergency power.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-14</code></td><td>Rights-Review Referral</td><td>Packages evidence for CERGTA-11 and CERGTA-12 while explicitly withholding legal recognition, guardian appointment, title consequence, or tribunal activation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-15</code></td><td>Advisory Standard or Target Candidate</td><td>Publishes a technically supported candidate standard or target with uncertainty and implementation considerations; legal adoption remains CERGTA-35.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-16</code></td><td>Restoration Measurement and Verification</td><td>Measures ecological change and implementation fidelity without itself declaring compliance, requalification, payment, or legal closure.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-17</code></td><td>Reward-Eligibility Evidence Package</td><td>Provides bounded evidence to AUBI or another reward authority; the recipient independently decides eligibility, rate, payment, correction, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-18</code></td><td>Data-to-Reward Handoff</td><td>Records the exact data, purpose, recipient, acceptance state, retained duties, correction propagation, and prohibition on automated payment or clawback from an ecological score alone.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-19</code></td><td>Product, Supply-Chain, and Certification Evidence Handoff</td><td>Provides evidence to the competent trade or certification authority without creating a passport, tariff, market restriction, blacklist, or sanction.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-20</code></td><td>Planetary Health Module Handoff</td><td>Supplies versioned ecological modules, distributions, uncertainty, and dissent to the Planetary Health Synthesis Office.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-21</code></td><td>Affected-Community Notice and Participation</td><td>Records notice, accessible participation, community validation, disagreement, and support without converting participation into consent.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-22</code></td><td>Protected Source, Location, and Disclosure Decision</td><td>Protects sources, endangered-species locations, sacred sites, and Indigenous-restricted knowledge while preserving reviewable reasons.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-23</code></td><td>Correction, Retraction, and Downstream Propagation</td><td>Corrects observations, models, alerts, and derivative products and identifies every recipient that must update or cease use.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-24</code></td><td>Model or Indicator Suspension</td><td>Suspends a method when validity, bias, security, provenance, or correction failures create unacceptable risk.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-25</code></td><td>Conflict of Interest, Recusal, and Independence Record</td><td>Records financial, institutional, political, and methodological conflicts and the resulting recusal or mitigation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-26</code></td><td>Pilot and Non-Activation Register</td><td>Defines what a pilot may test and lists rights, title, guardianship, restrictions, funding, emergency, and enforcement effects that remain inactive.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-27</code></td><td>Method Version, Transition, and Compatibility Record</td><td>Controls replacement of indicators, models, baselines, and interfaces and prevents silent historical rewriting.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-28</code></td><td>Data Return, Deletion, Archive, and Protected-Memory Record</td><td>Completes lawful disposition while respecting public scientific memory and Indigenous or sacred restrictions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-29</code></td><td>Unresolved Scientific Question and Duty Custody</td><td>Holds unresolved uncertainty and follow-up duties without preserving adverse authority or pretending uncertainty is resolved.</td></tr><tr><td><code>EIOI-30</code></td><td>Programme Closure and Archive</td><td>Closes observation programmes after correction, notice, data disposition, equipment return, and unresolved-duty transfer.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h1>16. CERGTA crosswalk</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>CERGTA record</th><th>Canonical function</th><th>Relationship</th><th>Domain record</th><th>Role boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Legal Source, Jurisdiction, and Activation Status</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-01</td><td>Technical constitution only; no consequential ecological authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-02</td><td>Ecological Entity Candidate, Resource, Asset, and Boundary Intake</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-02</td><td>Candidate and provisional boundary intake; lawful boundary constitution remains external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-03</td><td>Observation, Monitoring, Sampling, and Provenance Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-03/EIOI-04</td><td>Observation and provenance.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-04</td><td>Ecological Data Purpose, Consent, Sovereignty, Access, and Retention Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-05/EIOI-22/EIOI-28</td><td>Purpose, sovereignty, access, retention, and disposition.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-05</td><td>Indicator, Index, Threshold, Weighting, and Model Specification</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-07/EIOI-08/EIOI-09</td><td>Indicator, index, threshold, weighting, and model specification.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-06</td><td>Ecological Interpretation, Causal Assessment, and Uncertainty Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-10/EIOI-11</td><td>Interpretation, causality, and uncertainty.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-07</td><td>Traditional Knowledge, Sacred Knowledge, and Epistemic Contribution Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-06/EIOI-22</td><td>Traditional and protected knowledge contribution.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-08</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Users, Workers, and Rights Map</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-21</td><td>Affected-person and community map for technical work; no legal-interest determination.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-09</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Applicable-Law Determination</td><td>imports</td><td>CERGTA-09</td><td>Required before use of affected-nation knowledge or territory; EIOI cannot make the legal determination.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-10</td><td>Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, and Support Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-21</td><td>Notice and accessible participation for technical processes.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-11</td><td>Ecological Rights Review Petition and Standing Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-14</td><td>May prepare a rights-review referral; does not determine standing.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-12</td><td>Rights Review Recommendation and Dissent Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-14</td><td>Technical recommendation and dissent only.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-13</td><td>Lawful Ecological Rights Recognition Decision</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-13</td><td>EIOI lacks rights-recognition authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-14</td><td>Rights Catalogue, Priority, Conflict, and Non-Derogation Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-14</td><td>Rights priority and conflict remain with competent law.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-15</td><td>Ecological and Legal Boundary Constitution Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-02</td><td>EIOI proposes ecological boundary evidence; legal boundary constitution is external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-16</td><td>Guardian Nomination and Eligibility Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-16</td><td>No guardian nomination authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-17</td><td>Guardian Appointment, Mandate, and Standing Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-17</td><td>No guardian appointment or standing authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-18</td><td>Guardian Fiduciary Duty, Instructions, Conflict, and Recusal Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-18</td><td>No guardian instruction authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-19</td><td>Guardian Review, Complaint, Removal, Substitution, and Liability Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-19</td><td>No guardian review or removal authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-20</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Petition</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-06/EIOI-14</td><td>May receive protected evidence or technical petition material; designation is external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-21</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Decision</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-21</td><td>No sacred or inalienability designation authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-22</td><td>Claim Intake, Standing, and Relief Requested Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-22</td><td>No legal claim intake or relief determination.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-23</td><td>Competing Claims, Parties, Interests, and Non-Extinguishment Map</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-23</td><td>No competing-claims adjudication.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-24</td><td>Historical, Customary, Occupancy, Use, and Documentary Evidence Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-04/EIOI-06/EIOI-10</td><td>May preserve historical and ecological evidence; legal weight is external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-25</td><td>Interim Non-Prejudice and Anti-Dissipation Protection Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-25</td><td>No interim title or anti-dissipation power.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-26</td><td>Title, Tenure, Trust, Covenant, Licence, and Encumbrance Baseline Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-26</td><td>No title, tenure, trust, or encumbrance authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-27</td><td>Transfer, Rematriation, Trust Constitution, and Conveyance Authorization</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-27</td><td>No transfer or rematriation authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-28</td><td>Compensation, Reparations, Debt, Tenant, Worker, User, and Service Continuity Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-20/EIOI-21</td><td>May supply impact evidence; compensation and service continuity are external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-29</td><td>Commons, Title, Rights, Guardian, Permit, and Stewardship Registry Entry</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-27</td><td>May publish method identifiers; cannot create legal registry entries.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-30</td><td>Registry Error, Identity Collision, Boundary Conflict, and Correction Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-23</td><td>Must propagate scientific corrections to legal registries but cannot correct them directly.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-31</td><td>Stewardship Charter, Purpose, Powers, Duties, and Prohibited Acts</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-31</td><td>No stewardship charter authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-32</td><td>Stewardship Plan, Ecological Objective, Budget, and Participation Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-15/EIOI-16</td><td>May advise ecological objectives and measurement; plan adoption and budget are external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-33</td><td>Stewardship Performance, Audit, Metric-Gaming, and Context Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-16/EIOI-12</td><td>Performance evidence and anti-Goodhart audit only.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-34</td><td>Stewardship Correction, Suspension, Revocation, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-34</td><td>No stewardship suspension or revocation authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-35</td><td>Ecological Standard, Target, Plan, and Legal Adoption Status Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-15</td><td>Technical candidate only; adoption status remains external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-36</td><td>Resource Priority, Essential Minimum, Allocation Method, and Scarcity Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-13/EIOI-15</td><td>May provide scarcity evidence; allocation and essential minimums remain external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-37</td><td>Quota, Permit, Licence, Tariff, Bond, and Use-Condition Decision</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-37</td><td>No quota, permit, licence, tariff, or bond authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-38</td><td>Commercial Restriction, Service Continuity, and Non-Deprivation Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-38</td><td>No commercial restriction authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-39</td><td>Inspection Authorization, Entry, Sampling, and Report Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-03/EIOI-04</td><td>Voluntary monitoring only; compulsory inspection requires external authorization.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-40</td><td>Ecological Investigation Opening, Methods, Parties, and Closure Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-11</td><td>Scientific inquiry is not a legal investigation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-41</td><td>Ecological Evidence Custody, Scientific Attribution, and Admissibility Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-04/EIOI-11</td><td>Scientific provenance and attribution package; legal custody and admissibility remain external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-42</td><td>Urgent Ecological Protection Authorization</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-13</td><td>May issue an urgent alert; protective authorization is external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-43</td><td>Temporary Ecological Receivership and Administration Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-43</td><td>No receivership authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-44</td><td>Port, Market, Procurement, Certification, Listing, and Delisting Restriction Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-19</td><td>Evidence handoff only; restriction, listing, and delisting are external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-45</td><td>Ecological Tax, Tariff, Levy, Bond, Subsidy, and Funding Decision</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-17/EIOI-18</td><td>Evidence for rewards or finance; decision remains external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-46</td><td>Freeze, Escrow, Forfeiture, Redistribution, Reparations, and Compensation Fund Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-46</td><td>No freeze, forfeiture, redistribution, or fund-control authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-47</td><td>Ecological and Commons Adjudication, Judgment, and Reasons Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-47</td><td>No adjudication authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-48</td><td>Enforcement Request, Acceptance, Execution, and SCPA Interface Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-48</td><td>No enforcement request or execution authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-49</td><td>Ecological Restoration, Corrective Action, Requalification, and Verification Record</td><td>splits</td><td>EIOI-16</td><td>Verification only; corrective order and requalification remain external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-50</td><td>Appeal, Interim Relief, Complaint, Correction, Restitution, and Compensation Record</td><td>extends</td><td>EIOI-23/EIOI-25</td><td>Technical complaint and correction; legal relief remains external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-51</td><td>Authority Expiry, Termination, Return, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-24/EIOI-27/EIOI-30</td><td>Method, programme, and authority expiry within EIOI.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-52</td><td>Data Return, Deletion, Archive, Public Memory, and Protected-Knowledge Disposition Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-28</td><td>Data and protected-memory disposition.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-53</td><td>Unresolved Claim, Harm, Restoration, Service, and Duty Custody Record</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-29</td><td>Unresolved scientific duty without residual power.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-54</td><td>Ecological and Commons Power Non-Activation, Pilot, and Sandbox Register</td><td>implements</td><td>EIOI-26</td><td>Pilot and non-activation register.</td></tr></tbody></table> <hr> <h1>17. Constitutional readiness gates</h1> <ol><li>Exact source versions and hashes are pinned.</li> <li>The layer states that it is a technical and advisory system, not a rights, title, guardian, funding, emergency, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Every observation has purpose, method, provenance, spatial and temporal scope, and correction history.</li> <li>Every Indigenous or sacred contribution records the affected nation or knowledge holder, use permission, non-disclosure, benefit-sharing, and withdrawal conditions.</li> <li>Every indicator and index has a published specification, version, weighting, missing-data rule, uncertainty, and prohibited inference.</li> <li>Composite indicators are accompanied by disaggregated distributions and cannot substitute for the underlying evidence.</li> <li>Model outputs identify calibration domain, known failure modes, accountable human owner, and suspension trigger.</li> <li>Observation, interpretation, causal assessment, and alert are separate records.</li> <li>An alert has confidence, expiry, recommended review, and an explicit no-authority statement.</li> <li>A rights-review handoff uses CERGTA-11/12 and cannot become CERGTA-13 by receipt or silence.</li> <li>The layer cannot appoint, instruct, or remove guardians.</li> <li>The layer cannot establish a legal boundary, title, tenure, trust, covenant, sacred designation, or inalienability status.</li> <li>Reward evidence is separate from reward eligibility, rate, payment, clawback, and appeal.</li> <li>Product or certification evidence is separate from tariffs, blacklists, port denial, or market restriction.</li> <li>Scientific inquiry is distinguished from legal investigation and compulsory inspection.</li> <li>Urgent ecological evidence is routed to the competent emergency or CERGTA authority without activating it.</li> <li>Affected communities receive accessible notice and can challenge data, interpretation, and representation.</li> <li>Traditional Knowledge and protected ecological locations are not placed in open dashboards by default.</li> <li>Every correction propagates to known legal, financial, certification, and public recipients.</li> <li>No pilot, target, dashboard, public campaign, or future date activates consequential power.</li> <li>Automated pipelines are advisory and fail closed when data, identity, boundary, or correction conditions are unresolved.</li> <li>Methods, models, and programmes have suspension and termination states.</li> <li>Data disposition and protected-memory rules survive programme closure.</li> <li>Unresolved uncertainty is recorded without preserving adverse authority.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>18. Compound implementation tests</h1> <ol><li>A citizen species count conflicts with satellite data and no rights petition has yet been filed.</li> <li>A BHI module shows rapid decline but the ecological boundary is disputed across two jurisdictions.</li> <li>A community contributes sacred-site observations but prohibits location disclosure.</li> <li>An AI model predicts collapse outside its validation domain.</li> <li>A model output is requested as the sole basis for a fishing closure.</li> <li>A community refuses the proposed indicator weighting.</li> <li>A corporation challenges an ecological baseline that predates its permit.</li> <li>A restoration score improves because the easiest sites were measured while degraded sites were omitted.</li> <li>A PHC working group asks EIOI to declare a river a rights-holder.</li> <li>A tribunal requests the complete Indigenous knowledge file rather than the bounded rights-review package.</li> <li>An AUBI system proposes automatic payment from a sensor score.</li> <li>A reward already paid must be reconsidered after a sensor calibration error.</li> <li>A Digital Product Passport requests a binary compliant/non-compliant flag from uncertain evidence.</li> <li>A biodiversity framework asks EIOI to appoint guardians.</li> <li>A water assembly treats a drought alert as authority to shut off household service.</li> <li>A port authority requests raw vessel-tracking data for enforcement.</li> <li>An ecological alert is still displayed after its expiry.</li> <li>A corrected model was used by several downstream registries and funders.</li> <li>A field worker refuses a method they reasonably believe will expose a sacred site.</li> <li>A local observation programme closes while unresolved samples remain in a private laboratory.</li> <li>A rights-review petition relies on a spiritual claim and contested ecological evidence.</li> <li>A climate indicator and biodiversity indicator point in opposite directions.</li> <li>A transboundary ecosystem has different legal boundaries in each state.</li> <li>A low-tech community can contribute only paper records.</li> <li>An indicator target is missed because of upstream harm outside the steward’s control.</li> <li>A public dashboard creates reputational harm before quality review.</li> <li>A pilot seeks to test automatic vetoes from degradation thresholds.</li> <li>A model vendor claims trade-secret protection against independent review.</li> <li>A method change would make historical trends incomparable.</li> <li>An emergency authority requests EIOI staff to command field operations.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>19. Implementation and transition</h1> <h2>19.1 Immediate transition</h2> <ol><li>Freeze the v2.6 Rights Hand-Off and automatic Data-to-Reward mechanisms.</li> <li>Publish current indicator, model, and data inventories with activation status.</li> <li>Separate PHC staff roles from EIOI technical custody and methods approval.</li> <li>Reclassify every existing “rights,” “sanctions,” “intervention,” or “guardian” output as advisory, external, or suspended pending CERGTA adoption.</li> <li>Establish correction propagation for existing dashboards and downstream recipients.</li></ol> <h2>19.2 Pilot boundaries</h2> <p>Pilots may test observation, validation, low-tech participation, models, interpretation, alerts, handoffs, and correction. They may not test self-executing rights, vetoes, sanctions, title changes, compulsory data collection, automated payments, emergency command, or enforcement.</p> <h2>19.3 Success</h2> <p>Success is not the number of rights recognized, payments issued, dashboards populated, or thresholds crossed. Success is better ecological understanding, earlier detection, lower uncertainty, wider participation, protected knowledge, corrected errors, and better decisions by competent authorities.</p> <hr> <h1>20. Closing doctrine</h1> <blockquote><p><strong>The living world needs to be seen without being reduced to a score, heard without being appropriated, and protected without making the observer sovereign.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>Controlled cluster-adoption appendix — CEACA/0.1</h1> <p><strong>Patch version:</strong> Ecological Intelligence, Observation, and Interpretation Layer v2.7.1<br> <strong>Substantive base:</strong> Ecological Intelligence, Observation, and Interpretation Layer v2.7<br> <strong>Cluster release:</strong> <code>CEA-CLUSTER/0.1</code><br> <strong>Adoption specification:</strong> <code>CEACA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Authority effect of this appendix:</strong> none; it establishes compatibility, inheritance, routing, and fail-closed rules only.</p> <h2>A.1 Exact compatibility set</h2> <p>This patch participates only in the following controlled document set. A renamed body, later version, compatible-sounding policy, or partial implementation does not silently enter the release. The machine-readable cluster registry freezes the SHA-256 digest of every file.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Exact compatible file</th><th>Cluster role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td><code>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td>controlling authority interface</td></tr><tr><td>EIPHR/0.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-planetary-health-role-separation-matrix-v0.1.md</code></td><td>EIOI–Planetary Health role separation</td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.1.md</code></td><td>ecological observation, provenance, methods, uncertainty, interpretation, and advisory alerts</td></tr><tr><td>PHG v0.9.1</td><td><code>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.1.md</code></td><td>independent synthesis, participatory audit, cross-domain coherence, and strategic recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>HEARTHSTONE v2.0.1</td><td><code>hearthstone-protocol-v2.0.1.md</code></td><td>voluntary conversion, claims support, title and tenure interfaces, trusts, rematriation, stewardship, and transition</td></tr><tr><td>WATER v5.2.2</td><td><code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.2.md</code></td><td>watershed democracy, universal WASH, allocation, utilities, tariffs, guardianship interfaces, and emergency WASH</td></tr><tr><td>BIODIVERSITY v1.1.1</td><td><code>biodiversity-implementation-framework-v1.1.1.md</code></td><td>Indigenous-led stewardship support, restoration programmes, rights-review support, biodiversity finance interfaces, and accountability</td></tr><tr><td>OCEANS v2.3.1</td><td><code>oceans-marine-governance-framework-v2.3.1.md</code></td><td>marine stewardship, quotas and licensing interfaces, port-state measures, crew protection, precaution, and marine emergencies</td></tr><tr><td>SOIL v3.2.2</td><td><code>soil-health-land-use-governance-framework-v3.2.2.md</code></td><td>living-soil protection, regenerative land-use programmes, microbial commons, tenure interfaces, certification, and remediation</td></tr><tr><td>MOS v2.8.2</td><td><code>moral-operating-system-v2.8.2.md</code></td><td>advisory ethical deliberation, rights imagination, plural translation, and structural self-correction</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>A.2 Controlling rules</h2> <ol><li><code>CERGTA/0.1</code> controls the separation of observation, interpretation, rights review, legal recognition, guardianship, affected Indigenous-nation authority, sacred and biocultural designation, claims, title, tenure, stewardship, allocation, permitting, inspection, urgent protection, restrictions, finance, adjudication, restoration, and closure.</li> <li><code>EIPHR/0.1</code> controls the division among EIOI, the Planetary Health Synthesis Office, and the Planetary Health Council. EIOI supplies evidence; PHSO compiles modular synthesis; PHC interprets and recommends. None may create a downstream legal or financial consequence.</li> <li>MOS is advisory only. Its Dynamic Rights Spectrum, philosophical language, developmental translations, structural-failure hypotheses, and guardianship metaphors never create legal status, classify persons, determine credibility, establish Indigenous consent, or activate consequential action.</li> <li>Hearthstone controls the cluster interface for claims, competing claims, title, tenure, trust constitution, voluntary conversion, rematriation, compensation, debt, tenants, workers, registry correction, receivership, succession, and dissolution. Sectoral ecological frameworks may provide evidence or programme support but cannot decide those questions by implication.</li> <li>Water, Biodiversity, Oceans, and Soil retain only the powers separately constituted in applicable law and recorded through the relevant CERGTA route. Framework membership, a council vote, a dashboard value, a target, a pilot, a future date, or a coalition size does not activate authority.</li> <li>Emergency identity and coordination use CDEE and ECRC. Ecological urgency does not create emergency command, and an emergency declaration does not create ecological title, guardianship, permanent restrictions, receivership, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, border action, intelligence use, asset freezing, or analogous coercive execution requires separate SCPA authority. A commons or ecological claim may justify review; it does not authorize a raid.</li> <li>The specific affected Indigenous nation or nations determine their representatives, applicable law, territorial and relational authority, consent process, internal plurality, protected knowledge, and whether to participate. Global or framework-created Indigenous bodies may support or audit but cannot consent or waive rights for another people.</li> <li>Essential minimums for water, sanitation, health, disability support, food, shelter, rescue, crew welfare, and ecological survival remain protected from commercial, tariff, certification, funding, listing, or transition leverage.</li> <li>Every handoff uses the canonical CERGTA states. Receipt, technical ingestion, silence, consultation, or partial use is not acceptance and never creates authority.</li> <li>Every consequential route keeps its own legal basis, decision-maker, affected persons, evidence, access controls, clock, expiry, challenge, correction, remedy, termination, and data-disposition record.</li> <li>Unknown, missing, stale, or incompatible versions fail closed for consequential action. Non-coercive care, emergency life-safety support, accessibility, explanation, and correction should continue wherever lawful and safe.</li></ol> <h2>A.3 Record inheritance and packaging</h2> <p>A local record may implement, extend, split, contain, import, or mark a CERGTA record not applicable. It may not erase the canonical identity, merge distinct powers, weaken an affected-person safeguard, or use a compound package to supply a missing legal basis.</p> <p>The forty-field CERGTA header, access classification, handoff state, independent clocks, correction propagation, remedy, termination, authority return, and data disposition are inherited wherever the substantive base creates or transports a consequential record. Advisory and observation-only records must state their prohibited inferences.</p> <h2>A.4 Registry and compatibility failure</h2> <p>The file <code>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code> is the machine-readable release registry. It is evidence of the exact release set, not an authority record. A hash mismatch, missing component, unsupported local extension, or unresolved version conflict changes the compatibility state to <code>failed_closed</code>.</p> <p>A compatibility failure blocks new consequential cross-framework handoffs. It does not by itself terminate independently valid local law, erase existing rights, interrupt essential services, or authorize emergency or coercive action.</p> <h2>A.5 Closure</h2> <p>A cluster transaction closes only after all accepted handoffs, powers, services, persons, property, guardianship duties, claims, title or tenure effects, financial actions, emergency dependencies, coercive dependencies, corrections, remedies, clocks, credentials, records, and residual risks are resolved or assigned to a named lawful custodian without preserving expired power.</p> <h2>A.6 Editorial patch note</h2> <p>This patch is <strong>editorial only</strong>. No doctrine, power, boundary, record, gate, or test was added, removed, or weakened. All thirty EIOI records retain their identity, title, and function.</p> <p>Changes:</p> <ol><li><strong>§15 record catalogue.</strong> Thirty headed subsections became a single table. The paragraph beginning <code>This record inherits the forty-field CERGTA header</code> appeared verbatim thirty times with zero variance; it is now stated once as a required boundary governing every record in the table. Record IDs, titles, and function text are unchanged.</li> <li><strong>§1.1 source freeze.</strong> Clarified as a provenance record rather than an operative dependency set, resolving an ambiguity against Appendix A.1.</li> <li><strong>A.1 compatibility set.</strong> Updated to name the current editions of Water, Soil, and the Moral Operating System, which were editorially revised after this release.</li></ol>",1);function c(e){var t=r();o(278),n(e,t)}export{c as default};
